    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d397">

                <pb facs="0646" n="532" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg532"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a4/694</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Embassy to the Department of
                        State</hi><anchor n="44" xml:id="d397-f1"/></head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        type="received-date" when="1931-12-05">undated</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>His Majesty’s Government in the United Kingdom consider that the only practical
                    way of discussing naval disarmament generally is to assume that, so far as
                    parties to Part 3 of London Naval Treaty are concerned, the figures to be
                    inserted in the disarmament convention will be in accordance with the
                    limitations already agreed at the Washington and London Naval Conferences and
                    that the consequently resulting convention will be coterminous with the
                    Washington and London Naval treaties.</p>
                <pb facs="0647" n="533" xml:id="pg533"/>
                <p>They would be glad to learn whether this view is shared by United States
                    Government.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d397-f1" n="44" type="source" xml:id="d397-f1-content">
                <p>Handed to the Under Secretary of State by the British Ambassador on December 5,
                    1931.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
